A very common security concept used in homes is the house alarm. There are many types of house alarms, but they all operate on similar premises. The basic house alarm
uses infrared sensors to detect any movement in the house. When these sensors do detect movement, they trip the alarm. Some sensors can determine between pets and
humans, allowing for alarms to be set when pets are left at home, for example during the workday. Perimeter sensors can also be fitted to the alarm system. These
use vibration sensors on doors and windows to detect abnormally high levels of vibration, alerting to potential intruders before they break in. 
Tripping the alarm can either be silent or loud. A silent alarm will send an alert to a monitoring centre or a police station, allowing intruders to be caught in the 
act. A loud alarm however, will create a loud siren to sound when the sensors are tripped. This acts as both a deterrence, helping to remove intruders. It also alerts
anybody nearby that there is trouble, potentially quickening the response time, helping catch intruders.

